 ### 4.3 Recording the result
 
@@ -122,7 +122,9 @@ The reimplementation succeeded, and the reason it did is the more useful result:
 
 Python's `json.dumps(sort_keys=True)` sorts dictionary keys at every level of nesting. JavaScript's `JSON.stringify` sorts nothing; it emits object keys in insertion order. A port that reproduced the ring-building logic exactly and serialized with `JSON.stringify(obj, null, 2)` directly would have produced output that looks like a ring, parses as a ring, carries the same counts, and hashes to a different value on any object whose insertion order differs from sorted order, which, for this schema, is most of them. Neither implementation alone can detect this: each is internally self-consistent, and a single implementer re-running their own code will get their own answer back every time. Only a second, independent implementation exposes it, and only if that implementation is compared byte for byte rather than field for field. The reimplementation adds an explicit recursive key sort (every plain object's keys sorted, every array's own order left untouched) immediately before serialization, applied once at the top of the ring object so it reaches every nested level the way Python's single flag does in one pass. That one function is the entire fix; everything else in the builder can be wrong in some unrelated way and this would not catch it, which is a limit of the check as much as a description of it.
 
-The seam belongs to the specification, not to either implementer. Section 3.3 defines the canonical form by naming a language and a function call ("Python's `json.dumps` with these options") rather than by stating the rules the call happens to enforce. That definition was reproducible in practice, because both implementers happened to land on the same effective ruleset, but it was under-specified in principle, because nothing prevented a second implementer from landing on a different, equally reasonable one and never finding out. RFC 8785 exists for exactly this: to state key ordering, string escaping, number formatting, and whitespace independently of any runtime's defaults, so that "canonical" describes the bytes rather than a call site.
+The seam belongs to the specification, not to either implementer, and the gap is wider than "names a language instead of stating rules." NENRIN_SPEC_v1.md does not state the canonical form at all: no json.dumps, no sort, no mention of canonicalization anywhere in it. The rule lives in the reference builder's own docstring and the ring-v1 README — artifacts, not the specification. Section 3.3 of this paper, stating the rule as sorted keys at every level, two-space indentation, raw non-ASCII, and one trailing newline, independent of any language, is its first language-neutral statement. The specification did not under-specify the canonical form so much as omit it, and the artifacts carried it instead. That gap was reproducible in practice, because both implementers independently arrived at the same artifacts and the same effective ruleset, but it was invisible in principle: nothing in the document a reimplementer is told to build from says a canonical form exists to find. RFC 8785 exists for exactly this: to state key ordering, string escaping, number formatting, and whitespace independently of any runtime's defaults, so that "canonical" describes the bytes rather than a call site.
+
+Adopting it would not be neutral between the two implementations reported here. RFC 8785 Section 3.2.3 specifies sorting property names as arrays of UTF-16 code units, compared as unsigned integers, and states the reason plainly: this maps directly to the native string type in ECMAScript, not to Python's. On the key-collation axis of Table 3, the Node.js reimplementation's default behaviour is already RFC-8785-conformant by construction. The Python reference, whose `sort_keys=True` orders by Unicode code point, is the implementation that would have to change if a future revision of the specification adopted RFC 8785 as written, not the other way around, for any endpoint whose status or consent-source values ever leave the ASCII range.
 
 Table 3 lists the latent seams of this class and reports, from the reimplementation's own code, which are exercised by the August rings and which are not.
 
